Z||000010||Formula88||08-08-2001||10:35 AM||||Check out <A HREF="http://www.grc.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.grc.com</A>  .  He has a test on his site to see if your PC is secure, etc.  And suggestions to fix any security leaks.  He also has a less than stellar opinion of BlackIce.  According to him, it records attempts, but does little to prevent them from hitting your computer.  I don't know if that's true or not.  Still, it's a good site to check out.||||reg||6
Z||000011||84Bill||08-08-2001||12:43 PM||||Humm?<BR>BlackIce (depending on the setting) will halt traffic on that port until you either "Clear" the attack list or "Trust" the attacker. I ran a test running Microsoft ISA Server/Firewall and the result was not as good as I thought. Then I ran the same test with BlackIce ("paranoid" setting) on the same box and it stopped EVERYTHING. IMO it's one of the easiest to use. the only drawback is you must allow access in order to open the port to that spacific traffic (eg. Game servers, chat, FTP sites and a few others)<P>Infact we used Blackice at work to determine what servers on our network that were infected with the CodeRed worm. That was the nastiest bug I have ever seen.||||reg||14
Z||000012||FieroMaster88||08-08-2001||01:14 PM||||I will have to download blackice after work then.  SOunds like a good thing to have.  Thanks guys<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||1
Z||000013||thomas_l||08-08-2001||02:47 PM||||I've been using BlackIce for about a year and really pleased with it. In paranoid mode your computer pretty much "doesn't exist" on the network. If you do any gaming on your home LAN, that can cause problems unless you allow local addresses. Make sure you go into your router's advanced settings and trn on some anti-spoofing rules and close any ports you don't use. The less you have visible on the outside, the more likely your 133t h4ck0r neighbor will move on to an easier target. I've been pretty lucky or just don't have as many nosy neighbors as some folks do. Only one PC Anywhere scan in the last few months. I never see the lame stuff like port scans or NT browser chatter because my router is closed up tight - but then I never need to access things from outside.||||reg||1
Z||000014||overlord||08-08-2001||06:46 PM||||Ha i just went to that grc webstite to test my comp....They say they cant find some port...my computer deliberatly hides it or something...  which is weird since i have no  fierowall(firewall sorry) or whatever whatsoever on my DSL conncetion.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||overlord||08-08-2001||06:50 PM||||heres exctly what they said.."Your Internet port 139 does not appear to exist! <BR>One or more ports on this system are operating in FULL STEALTH MODE! Standard Internet behavior requires port connection attempts to be answered with a success or refusal response. Therefore, only an attempt to connect to a nonexistent computer results in no response of either kind. But YOUR computer has DELIBERATELY CHOSEN NOT TO RESPOND (that's very cool!) which represents advanced computer and port stealthing capabilities. A machine configured in this fashion is well hardened to Internet NetBIOS attack and intrusion. <BR> Unable to connect with NetBIOS to your computer.<BR>All attempts to get any information from your computer have FAILED. (This is very uncommon for a Windows networking-based PC.) Relative to vulnerabilities from Windows networking, this computer appears to be VERY SECURE since it is NOT exposing ANY of its internal NetBIOS networking protocol over the Internet. "<P>and like i said i have no firewall , hard or soft whatsoever. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||thomas_l||08-08-2001||08:09 PM||||It says that because you (wisely) don't have file & print sharing enabled, either on your PC if you're direct to the modem or on your router if you're using one. The SheildsUp site does a quick port scan and reports back. If you already have a router running, NetBIOS will probably be disabled by default. Bottom line, close it if your not using it, and be careful with stuff you do leave open and running. If you feel pretty manly after that grc.com test, I can ask the network engineers at work test yer shields  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||overlord||08-09-2001||09:51 AM||||acutally i DO have file sharing on....since i am on a lan i need to share my drives.  But i know a little trick..<P>and i dont have a router...im just using 2 nics to share dsl access.||||reg||1
Z||000018||84Bill||08-09-2001||09:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by overlord:<BR><B>acutally i DO have file sharing on....since i am on a lan i need to share my drives.  But i know a little trick..<P>and i dont have a router...im just using 2 nics to share dsl access.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A little trick????? ::waiting::<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||DRH||08-09-2001||09:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B> A little trick????? ::waiting::</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ditto...<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||thomas_l||08-09-2001||04:08 PM||||Not really a secret. If you use an O/S that supports multiple NIC's you can share the connection. The PC connected to the cable/DSL modem becomes the default gateway for all the other machines. Win 2K (and Me too I think) has it built in, Linux does it too but requires more skill, I don't know about Macs, and with NT you just use a proxy program. Either WinGate or MS Proxy.<P>For Win 9x or anything that won't support more than one NIC, there are USB network interfaces on the market - USB on the computer end and RJ-45 on the network end. Then you use something like WinGate to pipe packets between the USB and NIC ports.<P>I have been using a router (ISDN at first then I got cable) for the last 3 years so I haven't set up a proxy like that recently.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Songman||08-09-2001||08:21 PM||||Thomas, do you also share the bandwidth that way? <P>I know I can set up a network so that any of my computers can access the internet through my one cable modem but if my girlfriend is surfing on the other computer it slows me down. But for $5 per month I get an extra modem and a new IP for that computer and she has her own bandwidth and don't slow down my Fiero surfin...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000022||thomas_l||08-10-2001||08:45 AM||||$5/mo isn't bad, but do they hang it on the same phone line? You can get a DSL/cable router at most of the big box stores for just over $100. Linksys has one with built in wireless for just over $200. I bought my stuff before those all-in-ones came out. I have a Netgear RT314 router that has a 4 port 10/100 switch built in. It will do a full class C of NAT'd addressing using additional switches or hubs if you need to plug in more than 4 devices. I think most of them on the market will do the same.<P>If you're buying this stuff you ought to look on eBay. Even with the newer gear, people move into new homes that don't have high speed Internet and the stuff goes cheap. I just looked and there's more than a dozen just like mine going for $75 or less. I think mine was $159 new (I was in a BIG hurry to get one when the cable modem got installed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000023||overlord||08-10-2001||09:02 AM||||songman,<BR>u said if ur girlfriend is surfing on the other comp that it slows u down?!?!<P>weird...2 of us can both play quake3 at the same time with the exact same ping and no slow down whatsoever...<P>then again i dont have cable......but dsl if that matters?  Ive never really played around with cable modem.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Songman||08-10-2001||02:11 PM||||Cable works differently that dsl... Cable is faster than dsl to start with, but it is a shared bandwidth system. If you live in a neighborhood where there are lots of cable modems you will not be as fast as out here  where I live where no one else has one...<P>And especially if you run off the same IP apparently. I am not sure exactly why it is that way, but that is what the cable company says and my research online backs that up...  And for $5 a month it is worth it to me just for the extra IP I can use...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
